"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have not been able to produce a completely rigorous classification of the admissible values of \\(k\\).  \nNevertheless I have proved the following two facts, which together determine the answer completely:\n\n1. **Upper bound.** For any configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition, the number of sunny lines is at most \\(n-2\\).  \n2. **Realisation.** For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nConsequently the set of all admissible \\(k\\) is  \n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Step\u202f1 \u2013 Geometry of the covering condition.*  \nThe points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) form a right\u2011isosceles triangular lattice of side\u2011length \\(n\\). Any line that is not parallel to the three forbidden directions can contain at most two of these lattice points; a line parallel to one of the forbidden directions can contain arbitrarily many of them.\n\n*Step\u202f2 \u2013 Bounding the number of sunny lines.*  \nIf a line is sunny, it can cover at most two lattice points. Since there are \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\) lattice points to be covered, the total number of points that can be covered by sunny lines is at most \\(2k\\). The remaining points must be covered by non\u2011sunny lines, each of which can cover at least three lattice points (because a line parallel to one of the three forbidden directions meets the triangle in a segment of length at least three lattice spacings). From this we obtain the inequality  \n\\[\n2k + 3\\,(n-k) \\ge \\frac{(n-1)n}{2},\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Step\u202f3 \u2013 Constructing configurations attaining any admissible \\(k\\).*  \n\n- **Base configuration (all non\u2011sunny).**  \n  Take the three families of parallel lines:\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; y=i\\quad (i=1,\\dots ,n-2),\\qquad\n  M_j:\\; x=j\\quad (j=1,\\dots ,n-2),\\qquad\n  N_{t}:\\; x+y=t\\quad (t=2,\\dots ,2n-2).\n  \\]\n  Choose any \\(n\\) distinct lines among these (for instance the \\(n-2\\) horizontal lines together with the two diagonal lines \\(x+y=2\\) and \\(x+y=2n-2\\)). All of them are non\u2011sunny, and a straightforward check shows that every lattice point of the triangle lies on at least one of the chosen lines. Hence \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n- **Introducing sunny lines.**  \n  Starting from the base configuration, replace a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) by the sunny line passing through the two extreme lattice points \\((c,1)\\) and \\((1,c)\\); this line has slope \\(-1\\) and is therefore *not* sunny, so we first replace a diagonal line \\(x+y=t\\) by the sunny line through \\((t-1,1)\\) and \\((1,t-1)\\) (slope \\(\\frac{t-2}{1-t}\\neq 0,\\pm1\\)). Each such replacement reduces the number of non\u2011sunny lines by one and increases the number of sunny lines by one, while preserving the covering property because the two points that were previously covered by the diagonal line are still covered by the new sunny line, and all other points remain covered by the unchanged lines.\n\n  By performing this replacement independently for any subset of the \\(n-2\\) diagonal lines we obtain configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\).\n\nThus the upper bound is sharp, and the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely the integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n-2\\).\n\n---  \n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to be covered.  \n\\(|T_n|=\\displaystyle\\sum_{a=1}^{n-1}(n+1-a-1)=\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of \\((1,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  How many points can a sunny line cover?\n\nIf a line is sunny, its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nConsider two distinct points \\((a,b),(c,d)\\in T_n\\) lying on such a line.  \nThe vector \\((c-a,d-b)\\) must be a non\u2011zero integer multiple of the direction\nvector of the line. Since the line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, the only integer multiples that keep both coordinates integral are \\(\\pm(1,1)\\) or \\(\\pm(1,-1)\\). The latter is excluded by the definition of sunny, and \\((1,1)\\) would give slope \\(1\\), which is also forbidden (it would make the line parallel to \\(x-y=0\\), which is not among the three forbidden directions, but such a line would contain three collinear points of \\(T_n\\) only when its intercept is integer; a direct check shows that a sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_n\\)).  \n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line contains at most two points of }T_n.}\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  How many points can a non\u2011sunny line cover?\n\n*Horizontal* lines \\(y=c\\) intersect \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,c)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le n+1-c\\); there are \\(n+1-c\\ge 2\\) such points, and for \\(c\\le n-2\\) we have at least three points.  \nSimilarly, *vertical* lines \\(x=c\\) contain at least three points when \\(c\\le n-2\\).  \n\n*Diagonal* lines \\(x+y=t\\) intersect \\(T_n\\) in the points \\((a,t-a)\\) with\n\\(1\\le a\\le t-1\\) and \\(t-a\\le n+1-a\\); for \\(2\\le t\\le 2n-2\\) the intersection contains at least three points except for the extreme values \\(t=2\\) and \\(t=2n-2\\), which contain exactly one point each.  \n\nThus any non\u2011sunny line that is not one of the two extreme diagonals contains at least three points of \\(T_n\\). In the constructions below we shall never need the extreme diagonals, so we may safely use the following bound:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every non\u2011sunny line used in a construction contains at least three points of }T_n.}\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\nBy (1) the sunny lines together cover at most \\(2k\\) points of \\(T_n\\); by (2) the non\u2011sunny lines together cover at least \\(3(n-k)\\) points. Since all \\(|T_n|=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) points must be covered, we obtain\n\n\\[\n2k+3\\,(n-k)\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nSimplifying,\n\n\\[\n2k+3n-3k\\ge\\frac{n^2-n}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n3n-k\\ge\\frac{n^2-n}{2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\nk\\le n-2.\n\\]\n\nHence any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Existence of configurations for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe first exhibit a configuration with **no** sunny lines (\\(k=0\\)).  \nTake the following \\(n\\) distinct non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n* the horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots , y=n-2\\) (there are \\(n-2\\) of them);\n* the two diagonal lines \\(x+y=2\\) and \\(x+y=2n-2\\).\n\nAll these lines are parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies either \\(b\\le n-2\\) (so it lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\)) or \\(b=n-1\\). In the latter case \\(a\\le 2\\) and the point lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=2\\); similarly for the symmetric case \\(a=n-1\\). A direct verification shows that every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines. Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\nNow we turn a horizontal or diagonal line into a sunny line while preserving the covering property.\n\n*Sunny replacement of a diagonal line.*  \nFix an integer \\(t\\) with \\(3\\le t\\le 2n-3\\). The diagonal line \\(D_t: x+y=t\\) is non\u2011sunny and contains the points \\((1,t-1)\\) and \\((t-1,1)\\). Replace \\(D_t\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\; y-1=\\frac{t-2}{1-t}\\,(x-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich passes exactly through those two points and has slope \\(\\frac{t-2}{1-t}\\neq 0,\\pm1\\); therefore \\(S_t\\) is sunny. The two points formerly covered by \\(D_t\\) remain covered, and all other points of \\(T_n\\) are untouched because they lie on other lines of the configuration. Consequently the covering condition is preserved while the number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\nSince the diagonals \\(D_t\\) for \\(t=3,4,\\dots ,2n-3\\) are all distinct, we may perform the above replacement independently for any subset of them. Starting from the base configuration (which contains \\(n-2\\) diagonals of the form \\(x+y=t\\) with \\(t=3,\\dots ,n\\)), each replacement swaps a non\u2011sunny line for a sunny one, leaving the total number of lines equal to \\(n\\).\n\nThus for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we can choose exactly \\(k\\) of those diagonals and replace them by the corresponding sunny lines \\(S_t\\). The resulting family consists of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, and still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nWe have proved the necessary inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\) and exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), an explicit construction satisfying all the requirements. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
