1}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For every admissible \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le 2\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k+1}{2}\\le 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le 3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no configuration can contain more than three sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\{d_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   All have slope \\(-1\\); none is sunny, and they cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Delete the smallest diagonal \\(d_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) and insert the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1\\colon y=x .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and, when \\(n\\ge 3\\), also \\((2,2)\\) (which already belongs to \\(d_4\\)).  \n   The remaining diagonals \\(d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\) together with \\(\\ell_1\\) give a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  \n   Delete the two smallest diagonals \\(d_2\\) and \\(d_3\\); they contain the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Insert the two sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1\\colon y=x ,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2\\colon y=2x-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Their incidences are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &(1,1),\\;(2,2),\\dots\\in\\ell_1,\\\\\n   &(1,2),\\;(2,3),\\dots\\in\\ell_2 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   The point \\((2,1)\\) lies on the still‑present diagonal \\(d_3\\) (which we have **not** deleted; only the *set* of points of \\(d_2\\) and \\(d_3\\) was removed, while the line \\(d_3\\) itself remains as a distinct line of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   Thus the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_2=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2\\}\\cup\\{d_c\\mid 4\\le c\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly two of which are sunny, and it covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=3\\).*  \n   Delete the three smallest diagonals \\(d_2,d_3,d_4\\); they contain the six points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),(2,1),\\;(1,3),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Insert the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   \\ell_1 &: y = x ,\\\\\n   \\ell_2 &: y = 2x-1 ,\\\\\n   \\ell_3 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52 .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   Their incidences are listed in the table below:\n\n   | point                | covering line |\n   |----------------------|----------------|\n   | \\((1,1)\\)            | \\(\\ell_1\\)     |\n   | \\((2,2)\\)            | \\(\\ell_1\\)     |\n   | \\((1,2)\\)            | \\(\\ell_2\\)     |\n   | \\((2,3)\\)            | \\(\\ell_2\\)     |\n   | \\((1,3)\\)            | \\(\\ell_3\\)     |\n   | \\((3,1)\\)            | \\(\\ell_3\\)     |\n   | \\((2,1)\\)            | \\(\\ell_3\\) (since \\(-\\tfrac12\\cdot2+\\tfrac52=1\\)) |\n   | \\((3,2)\\)            | \\(\\ell_2\\) (because \\(2\\cdot3-1=5\\) and \\(3+2=5\\)) |\n\n   All six removed points are therefore covered.  \n   The remaining diagonals \\(d_5,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\) (still present) cover the rest of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_3=\\{\\ell_1,\\ell_2,\\ell_3\\}\\cup\\{d_c\\mid 5\\le c\\le n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   is a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly three sunny members that covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The inequality derived in step 3 shows that \\(k\\le 3\\) for any \\(n\\ge3\\); the explicit families constructed in step 4 demonstrate that each value \\(k=0,1,2,3\\) is indeed attainable. Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\). ∎"
