ext{ is the intercept}).\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\(b=n+1-a\\) (the largest possible value of the second coordinate for a given \\(a\\)) shows that for each **fixed sum** \\(s=a+b\\) the equation \\(y=mx+b_0\\) can have **at most one** solution \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) unless \\(m=-1\\) (the excluded case).  \n   The possible sums are the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); there are \\(n\\) of them.  \n   Moreover, if \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) are two points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the same sunny line, then  \n\n   \\[\n   a+b=a'+b'\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence a sunny line can meet at most one point on each diagonal \\(d_c\\).  \n   The diagonal \\(d_c\\) contains \\(c-1\\) points, and the largest diagonal is \\(d_{\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor+1}\\), which has exactly \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points.  \n   Consequently a sunny line can contain at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n\n   The line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) contains precisely the points \\((t,t)\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\); therefore the bound (1) is sharp and is attained only by \\(y=x\\). ∎\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Suppose we have a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   Start from the “diagonal family’’ \\(\\{d_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n   To obtain \\(\\mathcal L\\) we must delete some of those diagonals and replace each deleted diagonal by a new line; the number of deletions equals the number of insertions, namely \\(k\\).  \n   Let the deleted diagonals be \\(d_{c_1},\\dots ,d_{c_k}\\) with \\(2\\le c_1<\\dots <c_k\\le n+1\\).  \n   The total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that disappear after the deletions is  \n\n   \\[\n   P=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(c_i-1).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Each of the \\(k\\) newly inserted sunny lines can recover at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\) by Lemma 1, therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;P .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   The right‑hand side of (2) is maximised when the deleted diagonals are the *smallest* ones, i.e. when \\(\\{c_i\\}=\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\). In that case  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{\\max}= \\sum_{j=1}^{k}j=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (3) with (4) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\ge 1\\) we may divide by \\(k\\) and obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge\\frac{k+1}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For every admissible \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le 2\\); consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k+1}{2}\\le 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le 3 .\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no configuration can contain more than three sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Explicit constructions for \\(k=0,1,2,3\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_0=\\{d_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   All have slope \\(-1\\); none is sunny, and they cover every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Delete the smallest diagonal \\(d_2=\\{(1,1)\\}\\) and insert the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1\\colon y=x .\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(\\ell_1\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) and, when \\(n\\ge 3\\), also \\((2,2)\\) (which already belongs to \\(d_4\\)).  \n   The remaining diagonals \\(d_3,\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\) together with \\(\\ell_1\\) give a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=2\\).*  \n   Delete the two smallest diagonals \\(d_2\\) and \\(d_3\\); they contain the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Insert the two sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_1\\colon y=x ,\\qquad\n   \\ell_2\\colon y=2x-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Their incidences are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &(1,1),\\;(2,2),\\