 order that every point of \\(S_n\\) be covered we need  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;P .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   To make (5) as easy as possible we should delete the *smallest* diagonals, because they contain the fewest points.  \n   Thus the worst‑case (largest) value of \\(P\\) under the constraint “exactly \\(k\\) diagonals are deleted’’ is obtained when  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{c_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}=\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting into (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{\\max}= \\sum_{j=1}^{k}j=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (5) with (6) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k\\ge 1\\) we may divide by \\(k\\) and obtain the necessary condition  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k+1}{2}\n   \\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\n   k\\;\\le\\;2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no configuration can contain more than** \\(2\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Construction showing that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (7) is attainable.**  \n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 2m-1\\) we exhibit a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – delete the smallest diagonals.*  \n   Delete the diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{k+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of points removed is exactly the value \\(P_{\\max}\\) from (6), i.e. \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – introduce \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_t:\\; y = t\\,x-(t-1).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny because its slope \\(t\\) is a positive integer \\(\\neq 0\\) and \\(\\neq -1\\).  \n   The line \\(\\ell_t\\) passes through the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(2,t+1),\\;(3,2t+1),\\dots ,\\bigl(s,\\, (s-1)t+1\\bigr)\n   \\]\n\n   as long as the second coordinate does not exceed \\(n+1-s\\).  \n   Direct verification shows that the set of points of \\(S_n\\) with sum \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigcup_{t=1}^{k}\\bigl(\\ell_t\\cap S_n\\bigr).\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, for a given sum \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) write \\(c= t+1\\) with \\(t=c-1\\); then the point \\((1,c-1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{c-1}\\) and belongs to the deleted diagonal \\(d_c\\).  Conversely, each point of a deleted diagonal has a unique representation as \\((s,\\,c-s)\\) with \\(c\\le k+1\\); solving \\(c-s = t s-(t-1)\\) gives \\(t=c-1\\) and \\(s=1\\), showing that the point is on \\(\\ell_{c-1}\\).  Hence every point lost by the deletions is recovered by exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_t\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – keep the remaining diagonals.*  \n   The diagonals \\(d_{k+2},d_{k+3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\) are left untouched.  They are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and together with the \\(\\ell_t\\) they form a family of  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-(k)) = n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.\n\n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_k:=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{d_c\\mid k+2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) satisfies all requirements and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (7) gives a necessary condition for the existence of a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines, and the construction above shows that this condition is also sufficient.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le 2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n   In particular the maximal possible number of sunny lines equals \\(2\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor-1\\). ∎"
