            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\Bigr\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nIn particular the maximal possible value of \\(k\\) is \\(2\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor-1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **The required point set.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   d_c=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The line \\(d_c\\) has slope \\(-1\\) and contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points.  \n   The family \\(\\{d_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines and covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Points of \\(S_n\\) on a sunny line).**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a line that is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is sunny).  \n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover the bound is attained exactly by the line \\(\\ell\\colon y=x\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(\\ell\\) as \\(y=mx+b\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and \\(m\\) finite (the case of a vertical line is similar).  \n   If \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\) then \\(b=ma+b_0\\) for some intercept \\(b_0\\).  \n   Substituting \\(b=n+1-a\\) (the maximal possible second coordinate for a given \\(a\\)) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n+1-a = ma+b_0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (m+1)a = n+1-b_0 .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(m\\neq -1\\), equation (2) shows that the abscissa \\(a\\) of any point of \\(\\ell\\cap S_n\\) is uniquely determined by the value of the sum \\(a+b\\).  \n   Consequently two distinct points of \\(S_n\\) lying on the same sunny line must have **different** sums \\(a+b\\).  \n   The possible sums are the integers \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\); there are \\(n\\) of them, but a sunny line can meet at most one point on each diagonal \\(d_c\\).  \n   Hence the number of points of \\(S_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) cannot exceed the size of the *largest* diagonal that a sunny line is able to intersect.  \n\n   For a sunny line the equality \\(a+b=c\\) together with (2) forces  \n\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{c-b_0}{m+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(a\\) must be a positive integer, the step between two admissible sums is \\(|m+1|\\ge 2\\).  \n   Therefore a sunny line can meet at most every second diagonal, i.e. at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   of the diagonals, which proves (1).  \n\n   The line \\(y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) meets exactly the points \\((t,t)\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\); thus the bound is sharp and is attained only by \\(y=x\\). ∎\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Start from the “diagonal family’’ \\(\\mathcal D=\\{d_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n   Suppose we wish to obtain a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   We must delete \\(k\\) of the diagonals from \\(\\mathcal D\\) and replace each deleted diagonal by a sunny line; the number of deletions equals the number of insertions, namely \\(k\\).\n\n   Let the deleted diagonals be \\(d_{c_1},\\dots ,d_{c_k}\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\le c_1<c_2<\\dots <c_k\\le n+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of points of \\(S_n\\) that disappear after the deletions is  \n\n   \\[\n   P=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(c_i-1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1 each newly inserted sunny line can cover at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   Hence the \\(k\\) sunny lines can recover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  In order that every point of \\(S_n\\) be covered we need  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;P .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   To make (5) as easy as possible we should delete the *smallest* diagonals, because they contain the fewest points.  \n   Thus the worst‑case (largest) value of \\(P\\) under the constraint “exactly \\(k\\) diagonals are deleted’’ is obtained when  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{c_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}=\\{2,3,\\dots ,k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting into (3) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{\\max}= \\sum_{j=1}^{k}j=\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (5) with (6) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k\\ge 1\\) we may divide by \\(k\\) and obtain the necessary condition  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k+1}{2}\n   \\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\n   k\\;\\le\\;2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1 .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no configuration can contain more than** \\(2\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Construction showing that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (7) is attainable.**  \n\n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   For any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le 2m-1\\) we exhibit a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – delete the smallest diagonals.*  \n   Delete the diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   d_2,d_3,\\dots ,d_{k+1}.\n   \\]\n\n   The total number of points removed is exactly the value \\(P_{\\max}\\) from (6), i.e. \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}{2}\\).\n\n   *Step 2 – introduce \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   For \\(t=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_t:\\; y = t\\,x-(t-1).\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny because its slope \\(t\\) is a positive integer \\(\\neq 0\\) and \\(\\neq -1\\).  \n   The line \\(\\ell_t\\) passes through the points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),\\;(2,t+1),\\;(3,2t+1),\\dots ,\\bigl(s,\\, (s-1)t+1\\bigr)\n   \\]\n\n   as long as the second coordinate does not exceed \\(n+1-s\\).  \n   Direct verification shows that the set of points of \\(S_n\\) with sum \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigcup_{t=1}^{k}\\bigl(\\ell_t\\cap S_n\\bigr).\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, for a given sum \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\) write \\(c= t+1\\) with \\(t=c-1\\); then the point \\((1,c-1)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{c-1}\\) and belongs to the deleted diagonal \\(d_c\\).  Conversely, each point of a deleted diagonal has a unique representation as \\((s,\\,c-s)\\) with \\(c\\le k+1\\); solving \\(c-s = t s-(t-1)\\) gives \\(t=c-1\\) and \\(s=1\\), showing that the point is on \\(\\ell_{c-1}\\).  Hence every point lost by the deletions is recovered by exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_t\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – keep the remaining diagonals.*  \n   The diagonals \\(d_{k+2},d_{k+3},\\dots ,d_{n+1}\\) are left untouched.  They are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and together with the \\(\\ell_t\\) they form a family of  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-(k)) = n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.\n\n   Consequently \\(\\mathcal L_k:=\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\cup\\{d_c\\mid k+2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) satisfies all requirements and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (7) gives a necessary condition for the existence of a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines, and the construction above shows that this condition is also sufficient.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le 2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n   In particular the maximal possible number of sunny lines equals \\(2\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor-1\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
