            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have produced a complete and rigorous solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\Bigr\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\nIn particular the largest possible number of sunny lines equals  \n\\(2\\bigl\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor-1\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **The point set**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n   For each integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   d_c=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=c\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The lines \\(d_c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) are pairwise distinct, non‑sunny and together cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n2. **Lemma 1 (Maximum points of a sunny line).**  \n   Let \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line, i.e. \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\ell\\cap S_n|\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Moreover the bound is attained exactly by the line \\(\\ell\\colon y=x\\).\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(\\ell\\) in the reduced rational form  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{p}{q}x+b ,\\qquad p,q\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;q>0,\\;\\gcd(p,q)=1,\n   \\]\n\n   with the restrictions \\(p\\neq 0\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would be horizontal) and\n   \\(p\\neq -q\\) (otherwise \\(\\ell\\) would be parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\ell\\cap S_n\\).  Substituting \\(b\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   a+\\frac{p}{q}a+b = a\\Bigl(1+\\frac{p}{q}\\Bigr)+b = a\\frac{p+q}{q}+b = s,\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   where \\(s:=a+b\\) is the sum of the coordinates of the point.  \n   Rearranging (2) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a = \\frac{q}{p+q}\\,(s-b).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(a\\) is an integer, the right–hand side shows that the\n   quantity \\(s\\) must be congruent to the fixed integer \\(b\\) modulo\n   \\(|p+q|\\); in other words  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\equiv b\\pmod{|p+q|}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently two distinct points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on the same sunny\n   line must have sums \\(s\\) belonging to different residue classes\n   modulo \\(|p+q|\\).  Since the possible sums are the integers\n   \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) (there are \\(n\\) of them), the line can meet at most\n   one point for each admissible residue class.  Hence the number of\n   points of \\(S_n\\) on \\(\\ell\\) is bounded by  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;+\\;1\n   \\;=\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+|p+q|}{|p+q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\;\\le\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(|p+q|\\ge 2\\) (the equality \\(|p+q|=1\\) would force\n   \\(p+q=\\pm1\\), which together with \\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) would give\n   \\(p=0\\) or \\(p=-q\\), both excluded).  This proves (1).\n\n   For the line \\(y=x\\) we have \\(p=q=1\\), hence \\(|p+q|=2\\) and the\n   congruence condition (4) becomes \\(s\\equiv b\\pmod 2\\).  The points of\n   \\(S_n\\) with even sum are exactly \\((t,t)\\) for\n   \\(1\\le t\\le\\bigl\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\); they all lie on\n   \\(y=x\\).  Thus the bound is attained, and the only way to attain it is\n   \\(|p+q|=2\\), i.e. \\(p=q=1\\), which gives the line \\(y=x\\). ∎\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Start from the family of non‑sunny diagonals\n   \\(\\mathcal D=\\{d_c\\mid 2\\le c\\le n+1\\}\\) (there are exactly \\(n\\) of\n   them).  Suppose we wish to obtain a configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n   We must delete \\(k\\) diagonals from \\(\\mathcal D\\) and replace each\n   deleted diagonal by a sunny line; the number of deletions equals the\n   number of insertions, namely \\(k\\).\n\n   Let the deleted diagonals be\n   \\(d_{c_1},\\dots ,d_{c_k}\\) with\n   \\(2\\le c_1<c_2<\\dots <c_k\\le n+1\\).\n   The diagonal \\(d_c\\) contains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\); hence\n   the total number of points that disappear after the deletions is  \n\n   \\[\n   P=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(c_i-1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1 each newly inserted sunny line can cover at most\n   \\(\\bigl\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  Therefore the\n   \\(k\\) sunny lines can recover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   points.  In order that every point of \\(S_n\\) be covered we need  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;P .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   To make (7) hardest to satisfy (i.e. to obtain the strongest necessary\n   condition on \\(k\\)) we should delete the **largest** diagonals,\n   because they contain the most points.  Hence the worst‑case choice is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{c_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}= \\{\\,n+1-k+1,\\;n+1-k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting this choice into (5) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{\\max}= \\sum_{j=n+1-k+1}^{n+1}(j-1)\n           = \\sum_{t=0}^{k-1}(n-t)\n           = kn-\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}.\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   Inserting (8) into (7) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;\\ge\\;\n   kn-\\frac{k(k-1)}{2}.\n   \\]\n\n   Dividing by \\(k\\;(k\\ge1)\\) and rearranging we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k-1}{2}\\;\\le\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\;\\le\\;2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1 .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Inequality (9) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a\n   configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   m:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   Let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le 2m-1\\).  \n   We shall exhibit a family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n   contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Step 1 – delete the largest diagonals.*  \n   Delete the diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   d_{n+1},\\;d_{n},\\;\\dots ,\\;d_{n+2-k}.\n   \\]\n\n   (If \\(k=0\\) nothing is deleted.)  The total number of points removed\n   is precisely the quantity \\(P_{\\max}\\) from (8).\n\n   *Step 2 – introduce \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   Write \\(k=r+s\\) where \\(0\\le r\\le m-1\\) and \\(1\\le s\\le m\\) (this\n   decomposition is always possible because \\(k\\le 2m-1\\)).  \n   Define the following two families of sunny lines:\n\n   * (i) **Slope 1 lines**  \n     \\[\n     \\ell_i:\\; y = x + (i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,r .\n     \\tag{10}\n     \\]\n     Each \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(1\\) (hence is sunny) and contains the\n     points of \\(S_n\\) whose difference \\(b-a = i-1\\).\n\n   * (ii) **Slope \\(-2\\) lines**  \n     \\[\n     \\rho_j:\\; y = -2x + (n+1+j),\\qquad j=1,2,\\dots ,s .\n     \\tag{11}\n     \\]\n     The slope \\(-2\\) is different from \\(0,-1,\\infty\\); therefore each\n     \\(\\rho_j\\) is sunny.  Solving the system\n     \\(\\begin{cases}a+b\\le n+1\\\\ b=-2a+n+1+j\\end{cases}\\) shows that\n     \\(\\rho_j\\) contains exactly the points of the deleted diagonals\n     whose sum equals \\(n+2-j\\).\n\n   *Verification of coverage.*  \n   - The diagonals that were **not** deleted, namely\n     \\(d_{2},d_{3},\\dots ,d_{n+1-k}\\), are kept unchanged; they cover all\n     points of \\(S_n\\) with sums \\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1-k\\).\n\n   - For a deleted diagonal \\(d_{n+2-t}\\) (where \\(1\\le t\\le k\\)) the\n     sum of its points equals \\(n+2-t\\).  If \\(t\\le r\\) then the line\n     \\(\\ell_t\\) (slope 1) contains the point \\((1,n+1-t)\\) of that\n     diagonal; because \\(\\ell_t\\) has slope \\(1\\), it also contains every\n     point of the diagonal whose coordinates differ by the same amount,\n     i.e. the whole diagonal \\(d_{n+2-t}\\).  If \\(t>r\\) write\n     \\(t=r+u\\) with \\(1\\le u\\le s\\); then the line \\(\\rho_u\\) (slope\n     \\(-2\\)) passes through the unique point of \\(d_{n+2-t}\\) with\n     abscissa \\(a = u\\) and therefore also covers the entire diagonal\n     \\(d_{n+2-t}\\).  In each case the deleted diagonal is completely\n     recovered by exactly one of the newly introduced sunny lines.\n\n   - The families (10) and (11) are pairwise disjoint and also disjoint\n     from the retained diagonals, because they have slopes \\(1\\) and\n     \\(-2\\) whereas the diagonals have slope \\(-1\\).  Hence the total\n     number of distinct lines is  \n\n     \\[\n     r+s + (n-k) = k + (n-k)=n .\n     \\]\n\n   Thus the constructed set \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct\n   lines, contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, and covers every point of\n   \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (9) gives a necessary condition for the existence of a\n   configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines, and the construction above shows\n   that the condition is also sufficient.  Therefore the admissible\n   values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;0\\le k\\le 2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n   The maximal possible number of sunny lines equals\n   \\(2\\bigl\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\bigr\\rfloor-1\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder