\\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nThen there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) that contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nChoose a real number \\(\\alpha\\) such that  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\notin\\{0,-1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\ns_i:\\; y=\\alpha (x-i)+i .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(s_i\\) passes through the lattice point \\((i,i)\\) and has slope \\(\\alpha\\); therefore it is sunny.  \n\nIf \\(i\\neq j\\) then the intersection \\(s_i\\cap s_j\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl(\\frac{i-j}{\\alpha+1},\\;\\frac{\\alpha i-j}{\\alpha+1}\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\nwhich is **not** a point of \\(T_n\\) because its coordinates are not both positive integers (the denominator \\(\\alpha+1\\neq0\\) and the numerator is not a multiple of \\(\\alpha+1\\)). Consequently the sets  \n\n\\[\nS_i:=s_i\\cap T_n\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise disjoint.\n\nNow we take the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines from the non\u2011sunny family used in Lemma\u202f1, but we must avoid the vertical lines already replaced by sunny ones. Define  \n\n\\[\nv_j:\\;x=j\\qquad\\text{for }j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\setminus\\{1,\\dots ,k\\},\n\\]\n\\[\nd:\\;x+y=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\((n-1)-k+1=n-k\\) such lines; they are all non\u2011sunny and distinct (the vertical lines have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, and the diagonal line has a different slope).\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\cup\\{v_j\\mid j\\notin\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\}\\cup\\{d\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe verify that \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers \\(T_n\\).\n\n*Case 1: points on the diagonal.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b=n+1\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(d\\).\n\n*Case 2: points with \\(a\\le k\\) and \\(a+b<n+1\\).*  \nSuch a point has the same first coordinate as the sunny line \\(s_a\\). Because \\(s_a\\) passes through \\((a,a)\\) and has slope \\(\\alpha\\neq -1\\), moving along \\(s_a\\) changes the sum \\(x+y\\) by the factor \\(\\alpha+1\\neq0\\); therefore every integer point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le n\\) lies on \\(s_a\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\in s_a\\).\n\n*Case 3: points with \\(a>k\\) and \\(a+b<n+1\\).*  \nHere \\(a\\) is not among \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\), so the vertical line \\(v_a\\) is present in \\(\\mathcal L\\) and contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every element of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThe lines are distinct: the sunny lines have slope \\(\\alpha\\) while all other lines are vertical or diagonal; among the vertical lines the \\(x\\)-coordinates differ; the diagonal line has a different slope from all others.  \n\nHence \\(\\mathcal L\\) is a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Necessity of at least two non\u2011sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Lemma 3}}\n\\]\n\n*Statement.*  In any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) there are **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines. Consequently \\(k\\le n-2\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that a covering family \\(\\mathcal F\\) contains at most one non\u2011sunny line.\n\nRecall that a non\u2011sunny line is either horizontal, vertical, or diagonal.  \nObserve the following facts about the set \\(T_n\\):\n\n1. **Every point \\((i,i)\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) lies on a line of slope \\(-1\\) or on a vertical line.**  \n   Indeed, the diagonal line through \\((i,i)\\) is \\(x+y=2i\\); this line has slope \\(-1\\) and belongs to the forbidden family. If we do not use that diagonal, the only way to cover \\((i,i)\\) with a non\u2011sunny line is the vertical line \\(x=i\\).\n\n2. **A sunny line can contain at most one point of the form \\((i,i)\\).**  \n   Suppose a sunny line \\(L\\) (slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)) contains two distinct points \\((i,i)\\) and \\((j,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\). Then the slope of the segment joining them is \\(\\frac{j-i}{j-i}=1\\), contradicting the definition of sunny. Hence each sunny line contributes at most one such diagonal point.\n\nNow count the points \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\). There are \\(n\\) of them.  \n\nIf \\(\\mathcal F\\) has **no** non\u2011sunny line, then all these points must be covered by sunny lines, each providing at most one point; therefore at least \\(n\\) sunny lines are needed, contradicting the fact that \\(|\\mathcal F|=n\\) (the remaining points of \\(T_n\\) would have nowhere to be placed).  \n\nIf \\(\\mathcal F\\) has **exactly one** non\u2011sunny line, denote it by \\(L\\).  \nIf \\(L\\) is vertical, say \\(x=c\\), then it can cover at most one of the points \\((i,i)\\) (namely \\((c,c)\\)).  \nIf \\(L\\) is diagonal, it can cover at most one of them as well (the one with \\(2i=c\\)).  \nIf \\(L\\) is horizontal, it does not pass through any \\((i,i)\\) with \\(i\\ge1\\).  \n\nThus the single non\u2011sunny line contributes at most one of the \\(n\\) diagonal points, leaving at least \\(n-1\\) of them to be covered by sunny lines. Since each sunny line can cover at most one such point, we would need at least \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Together with the one non\u2011sunny line this gives at least \\(n\\) lines, leaving no line left to cover the remaining points of \\(T_n\\) that are not on the main diagonal. Hence a covering with only one non\u2011sunny line is impossible.\n\nTherefore any covering family must contain **at least two** non\u2011sunny lines. Consequently the number of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have a construction for \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma\u202f2 provides constructions for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nLemma\u202f3 shows that no covering can have \\(k=n-1\\) or \\(k=n\\).  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of 