und on \\(k\\)\n\nThere are exactly \\(n\\) lines in the configuration, hence the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A family of non‑sunny lines covering almost all points\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{ns}= \\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\) are parallel either to the \\(x\\)-axis or to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence they are **non‑sunny**.  \nThe number of lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\) is \\((n-k)+k=n\\).\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_{ns}\\).  \nIf \\(a>n-k\\) then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), and from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b\\le k\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_{ns}\\).\n\nConsequently **every** point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\) alone already satisfies the covering requirement, using exactly \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines and **zero** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Reducing the number of non‑sunny lines\n\nTo obtain configurations with a prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines we replace, one by one, some of the non‑sunny lines by sunny ones while preserving the covering property.\n\nAssume we have already constructed a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le s<k\\)).  \nPick a non‑sunny line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nU_\\ell=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid (a,b)\\text{ lies on }\\ell\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points of \\(T_n\\) that are covered **only** by \\(\