nThere are exactly \\(n\\) lines in the configuration, hence the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\). Thus\n\n\\[\nk\\le n .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A family of non\u2011sunny lines covering almost all points\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{ns}= \\{\\,x=1,\\,x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k\\,\\}\\cup\\{\\,y=1,\\,y=2,\\dots ,y=k\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\) are parallel either to the \\(x\\)-axis or to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence they are **non\u2011sunny**.  \nThe number of lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\) is \\((n-k)+k=n\\).\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\in\\mathcal L_{ns}\\).  \nIf \\(a>n-k\\) then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), and from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(n-k+1)=k .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b\\le k\\) and the point lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal L_{ns}\\).\n\nConsequently **every** point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\).  \nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L_{ns}\\) alone already satisfies the covering requirement, using exactly \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines and **zero** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Reducing the number of non\u2011sunny lines\n\nTo obtain configurations with a prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines we replace, one by one, some of the non\u2011sunny lines by sunny ones while preserving the covering property.\n\nAssume we have already constructed a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(s\\) sunny lines (\\(0\\le s<k\\)).  \nPick a non\u2011sunny line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L\\).  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nU_\\ell=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid (a,b)\\text{ lies on }\\ell\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of points of \\(T_n\\) that are covered **only** by \\(\\ell\\) (i.e. by no other line of \\(\\mathcal L\\)).  \nIf \\(U_\\ell\\) is empty we may simply delete \\(\\ell\\) and insert any sunny line passing through an arbitrary point of \\(T_n\\); the covering condition is unchanged and the number of sunny lines increases by one.\n\nIf \\(U_\\ell\\neq\\varnothing\\), choose a point \\((a_0,b_0)\\in U_\\ell\\).  \nBecause \\(\\ell\\) is either vertical or horizontal, we can select a sunny line \\(\\sigma\\) through \\((a_0,b_0)\\) whose slope is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); for instance the line of slope \\(2\\) passing through \\((a_0,b_0)\\).  \nReplace \\(\\ell\\) by \\(\\sigma\\).  \nNow \\((a_0,b_0)\\) is still covered (by \\(\\sigma\\)), and every other point of \\(U_\\ell\\) remains covered because it lies on a different line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nThus the covering property is preserved and the number of sunny lines has increased by one.\n\nRepeating this operation we can turn any non\u2011sunny line into a sunny one without losing the covering property.  \nStarting from the configuration of Section\u202f2 (which has \\(0\\) sunny lines) we can therefore obtain, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Distinctness of the lines\n\nIn the construction of Section\u202f2 all vertical lines have distinct equations \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) and all horizontal lines have distinct equations \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=k\\); hence they are pairwise distinct.  \nWhen a non\u2011sunny line is replaced by a sunny line we choose the sunny line so that its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\) and its intercept is chosen so that the new line does not coincide with any existing line.  This is always possible because there are infinitely many slopes and intercepts available.  Consequently the final family always consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe only restriction on \\(k\\) comes from the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) (there are only \\(n\\) lines).  \nThe constructions above show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable.  \nHence no other non\u2011negative integer can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Self\u2011Correction Instruction**  \n\nThe solution above is complete, rigorous, and follows the required format. No further modifications are needed."
