(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}+1\\), while the right‑hand side equals \\(\\frac{n(2n-2)}{2}\\).  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}+1 > \\frac{n(2n-2)}{2},\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts the inequality derived from the covering assumption.\n\nHence our initial assumption that a sunny line can belong to a covering family is false.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final conclusion\n\nNo covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines can contain a sunny line.  \nConsequently the only admissible number of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k=0}.\n\\]\n\nThis value is attainable, as shown in Section 1. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
