\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\)\nlies on the line \\(x=a\\in V_{n-k}\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The uncovered points\n\nThe points that are not yet covered are  \n\n\\[\nT_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a\\ge n-k+1\\}\n      =\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\mid n-k+1\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_k\\) is a right\u2011angled triangle with vertices  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(n-k+1,1\\bigr),\\qquad (n,1),\\qquad \\bigl(n-k+1,k\\bigr).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines of slope\u202f1 covering \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\; y = x - n + j .\n\\]\n\nAll \\(\\ell_j\\) have slope \\(1\\); consequently each \\(\\ell_j\\) is sunny.\nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) are distinct because their intercepts\n\\(-n+j\\) are distinct.\n\n*Claim.*  \\(\\displaystyle T_k=\\bigcup_{j=1}^{k}\\bigl(\\ell_j\\cap\\mathcal P_n\\bigr).\\)\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  Set \\(d=b-a\\).  From the description of \\(T_k\\),\n\n\\[\n1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n-n+1\\le d\\le -n+k .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\), the lower bound improves to \\(d\\ge -n+k\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nd\\in\\{-n+1,\\,-n+2,\\dots ,\\,-n+k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(d=-n+j\\) for a unique \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\), i.e. \\((a,b)\\) satisfies\n\\(b=a-n+j\\) and lies on \\(\\ell_j\\).\n\nConversely, fix \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\).  Any point of \\(\\ell_j\\) has the form\n\\((a,\\,a-n+j)\\).  The condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\na+(a-n+j)\\le n+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;2a\\le 2n+1-j,\n\\]\n\nwhich is equivalent to \\(a\\le n\\).  Together with the requirement \\(b\\ge1\\)\n(i.e. \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\)), we obtain exactly the description of the points of\n\\(T_k\\) that lie on \\(\\ell_j\\).  Hence every point of \\(\\ell_j\\) that belongs to\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is in \\(T_k\\).  \u220e\n\n#### 2.4  The whole family of lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k = V_{n-k}\\;\\cup\\;\\{\\ell_1,\\d