**  \n   Assume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfies the\n   covering condition and contains at least one sunny line \\(\\ell\\).  \n   Because \\(\\ell\\) is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\), its\n   slope \\(m\\) belongs to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\).\n\n   Consider the set of points of \\(S_{n}\\) that lie on \\(\\ell\\).  Since \\(\\ell\\) is not\n   vertical, at most one point of each column \\(\\{x=a\\}\\) can belong to \\(\\ell\\); similarly,\n   because \\(\\ell\\) is not horizontal, at most one point of each row \\(\\{y=b\\}\\) can belong\n   to \\(\\ell\\).  Moreover, because \\(\\ell\\) is not of slope \\(-1\\), it cannot contain two\n   points \\((a,b)\\) and \\((a',b')\\) with \\(a+b=a'+b'\\).  Consequently the number of points of\n   \\(S_{n}\\) that \\(\\ell\\) can cover is at most \\(n\\) (the size of a maximal set of points\n   with distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, distinct \\(y\\)-coordinates and distinct sums \\(x+y\\)).\n\n   The total number of points in \\(S_{n}\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_{n}|=\\binom{n+1}{2}= \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n\\qquad (n\\ge 3).\n   \\]\n\n   Removing the at most \\(n\\) points covered by the sunny line \\(\\ell\\) leaves at least  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\ge 3\n   \\]\n\n   points still to be covered.  The remaining \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are each\n   either vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\) (otherwise they would be sunny as well).\n   Any such line can contain at most one point from each column (or each row, or each\n   diagonal \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\) respectively).  A straightforward counting argument shows\n   that with only \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines it is impossible to cover the remaining\n   \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) points, because each non‑sunny line can cover at most \\(n\\) of them,\n   giving a total capacity of at most \\((n-1)n < \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\).  This\n   contradiction proves that no sunny line can appear in a covering family consisting of\n   exactly \\(n\\) lines.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction in (1) shows that \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.  \n   The impossibility argument in (2) shows that any covering family of exactly \\(n\\) lines\n   cannot contain a sunny line; therefore no value \\(k\\ge 1\\) can occur.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The only admissible value is }k=0.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
