e exactly the points \\((x,1+i(x-1))\\) with\n   \\[\n   1\\le x\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   Hence \\(\\ell_i\\) contains  \n   \\[\n   c_i:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\]\n   points of \\(T_n\\).  Summing over \\(i=1,\\dots ,m\\) we obtain a lower bound\n   \\[\n   C(m):=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}c_i\\ge \\frac{m(2n+1-m)}{2}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Inequality (1) follows from the elementary estimate\n   \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge n+1-i\\) for \\(1\\le i\\le m\\).\n\n3. **Vertical‑line completion.**  \n   After using the \\(m\\) sunny lines, at most\n   \\[\n   |T_n|-C(m)\\le\\frac{(n-m)(n-m+1)}{2}\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   points of \\(T_n\\) may remain uncovered (by (1) and the exact value of \\(|T_n|\\)).  \n   The \\(n-m\\) vertical lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-m\n   \\]\n   are non‑sunny and together contain exactly the number of points displayed in (2); consequently they can be chosen so as to cover all the still uncovered points.\n\n4. **Realising a given \\(k\\).**  \n   Take \\(m=k\\).  By step 2 we obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines covering at least the number of points given in (1).  By step 3 the remaining points (no more than the quantity in (2)) are covered by the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines.  Thus we have constructed \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, that cover every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n5. **Partial nature of the result.**  \n   The argument above guarantees the existence of a configuration with **at least** \\(k\\) sunny lines for each \\(k\\).  To turn “at least” into “exactly”, one would need to show that the vertical lines used in step 3 can be replaced, when necessary, by non‑sunny lines of other allowed slopes (horizontal or slope \\(-1\\)) without increasing the number of sunny lines.  This refinement has not been completed.\n\nConsequently we have proved that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is a *possible lower bound* for the number of sunny lines in a suitable configuration, and that no configuration can have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines.  The exact characterisation “\\(k\\) can be realised” remains open.  \n\n---  \n\n*Further work required:* a construction that, for each \\(k\\), uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (no more) while still covering all points of \\(T_n\\). This would close the gap and yield the full answer \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
