 \\]\n\n   A straightforward case analysis on the parity of \\(n\\) shows that this remainder is **strictly larger than \\(n\\)**, i.e. more than one non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points, so at least two non‑sunny lines would be required.  \n   Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), we would need at least \\((n-1)+2=n+1\\) lines, a contradiction.  \n   Therefore \\(k=n-1\\) cannot occur.\n\nCombining §§4–6 we have proved that the attainable values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \n|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Points on a sunny line  \n\nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line containing two distinct points of \\(T_n\\),\n\\((x_0,y_0)\\) and \\((x_0+p,y_0+q)\\) with integers \\(p,q\\neq0\\) and\n\\(p\\neq -q\\) (otherwise the slope would be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)).\nMoving from one lattice point of \\(\\ell\\cap T_n\\) to the next changes the sum\n\\(a+b\\) by \\(|p|+|q|\\ge 2\\).  \nSince every point of \\(T_n\\) satisfies \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\) can lie on a sunny line.  (If the line contains only one\npoint, the bound is trivially satisfied.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A necessary inequality for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nFrom (10) the sunny lines together cover at most  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_n\\).  \nConsequently at least  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\npoints remain uncovered and must be covered by non‑sunny lines.\nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) can contain at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\); therefore the number of non‑sunny lines needed is at\nleast  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|T_n|-k\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor}{n}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSince the total number of lines is exactly \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nk+\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{|T_n|-k\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor}{n}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le n .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nA direct verification (separating the cases \\(n\\) even and odd) shows that\n(14) fails **only when \\(k=n-1\\)**; for \\(k=n\\) the left‑hand side equals \\(n\\),\nand for every \\(k\\le n-2\\) the ceiling in (13) is at most \\(2\\), so (14) holds.\nThus\n\n\\[\nk=n-1\\quad\\text{is impossible}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Construction for \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y-1=i\\,(x-1).\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_i\\) has slope \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence it is sunny.\nA point \\((x,y)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\) iff \\(y=1+i(x-1)\\).\nSubstituting this into the defining inequality of \\(T_n\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx+1+i(x-1)\\le n+1\\iff (i+1)x\\le n+i .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(x\\) is a positive integer, the admissible values of \\(x\\) are  \n\n\\[\n1\\le x\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nThus the points of \\(T_n\\) on \\(\\ell_i\\) are exactly the lattice points described\nby (17).  For different indices \\(i\\) the corresponding sets are disjoint,\nbecause two distinct slopes cannot intersect the integer lattice in more than\none point.  Summing the cardinalities given by (17) over \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) yields\n\\(|T_n|\\); consequently the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) cover all\npoints of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nTake the first \\(k\\) lines of the family (16), i.e. \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\).\nFor each \\(i\\le k\\) let  \n\n\\[\nc_i:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+i}{i+1}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nFrom (17) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly \\(c_i\\) points of \\(T_n\\).\nA simple inequality\n\n\\[\nc_i\\ge n+1-i\\qquad(1\\le i\\le k)\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nholds because \\(\\frac{n+i}{i+1}=1+\\frac{n-i}{i+1}\\ge 1+\\frac{n-i}{n}\\) and the\nfloor of a number exceeding \\(n-i\\) is at least \\(n+1-i\\).\nSumming (18) gives  \n\n\\[\nC(k):=\\sum_{i=1}^{k}c_i\\ge\\sum_{i=1}^{k}(n+1-i)=\\frac{k(2n+2-k)}{2}.\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\n*Non‑sunny completion.*  \nThe number of points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered is at most  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|-C(k)\\le\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-\\frac{k(2n+2-k)}{2}\n=\\frac{(n-k)(n-k+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nThe right‑hand side of (20) equals \\(|T_{\\,n-k}|\\); therefore every uncovered\npoint \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(a\\le n-k\\).  We now cover all these points by the\n\\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\]\n\nThese lines are non‑sunny (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis) and are distinct from\nthe sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\) (which have non‑zero finite slopes).  Consequently we\nobtain a family of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of\nthem sunny, that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.\nUsing the bound (10) each sunny line contributes at most\n\\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points, so the sunny lines cover at most  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints.  The number of points still uncovered is therefore at least  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-1)\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nA short case check shows that this quantity exceeds \\(n\\) for every\n\\(n\\ge3\\); consequently more than one non‑sunny line would be required, which\ncontradicts the fact that the total number of lines is \\(n\\).  Hence no\nconfiguration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nAll integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) are realizable by the construction\nof §4, the integer \\(k=n\\) is realizable by the construction of §3, and the\ninteger \\(k=n-1\\) is impossible by §5.  Therefore the set of admissible\nvalues of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}} .\n\\]"
