define the line of slope \\(-1\\)\n\n\\[\n\\ell^{(-1)}_c:\\; x+y=c+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(\\ell^{(-1)}_c\\) contains exactly the points  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=c+1,\\; a\\ge 1,\\; b\\ge 1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThese are precisely the points of \\(T_n\\) whose sum of coordinates exceeds \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+1\\) but does not exceed \\(n+1\\).  Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates are both larger than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) lies on one of the \\(\\ell^{(-1)}_c\\).\n\nThe number of \\((-1)\\)-slope lines is  \n\n\\[\ns:=c_{\\max}=n-2\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Counting the lines\n\nThe total number of lines constructed is  \n\n\\[\nh+v+s\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n    +\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n    +\\bigl(n-2\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\bigr)\n   =n .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are distinct because they have different intercepts (for the horizontal and vertical families) or different constant terms (for the \\((-1)\\)-slope family).\n\nEach line belongs to one of the three forbidden directions:\n\n* \\(\\ell^{(h)}_b\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\n* \\(\\ell^{(v)}_a\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis,\n* \\(\\ell^{(-1)}_c\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nHence none of the lines is sunny.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Coverage of \\(T_n\\)\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(\\ell^{(h)}_b\\).\n\n*If \\(b>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) but \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)*, then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(\\ell^{(v)}_a\\).\n\n*If both \\(a,b>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)*, then \\(a+b\\ge 2\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor+2\\).  \nSince \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\na+b=c+1\\quad\\text{for some }c\\in\\{1,\\dots,c_{\\max}\\},\n\\]\n\nand consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(\\ell^{(-1)}_c\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of the set \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\), a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines such that\n\n* each line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, or the line \\(x+y=0\\) (hence none is sunny);\n* every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nTherefore the integer  \n\n\\[\nk=0\n\\]\n\nis always admissible.\n\nThe existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge 1\\) (i.e. containing at least one sunny line) has not been resolved by the present work.  Determining the full set of admissible values of \\(k\\) remains an open problem."
