aining them is  \n\n   \\[\n   S_s^{(1)}:\\; y = \\frac{t}{\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor-1}\\,x\n                -\\frac{t}{\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor-1}+1 . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Similarly the points of \\(P_s^{(2)}\\) lie on  \n\n   \\[\n   S_s^{(2)}:\\; y = \\frac{t}{\\lceil s/2\\rceil-1}\\,x\n                -\\frac{t}{\\lceil s/2\\rceil-1}+1 . \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   In (3)–(4) the denominators are positive (because \\(s\\ge4\\)), and the slopes are\n   positive rational numbers different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore both\n   \\(S_s^{(1)}\\) and \\(S_s^{(2)}\\) are **sunny**.  Moreover,\n   \\(S_s^{(1)}\\cup S_s^{(2)}\\) contains all points of \\(P_s\\), while the two endpoints\n   of (1) remain covered by the axis lines.\n\n   Hence we may **replace** the diagonal \\(D_s\\) by the two sunny lines\n   \\(S_s^{(1)},S_s^{(2)}\\) without losing any required point; the total number of\n   lines increases by one.\n\n4. **Obtaining exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.**  \n   Start from the base configuration of step 2 (which has \\(k=0\\)).  \n   Choose any \\(k\\) distinct diagonals \\(D_{s_1},\\dots ,D_{s_k}\\) with\n   \\(s_i\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and replace each of them by the two sunny lines\n   described in step 3.  After the replacements we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{2}_{\\text{axis}}+\n   \\underbrace{(n-2-k)}_{\\text{remaining diagonals}}+\n   \\underbrace{2k}_{\\text{new sunny lines}}=n+k\n   \\]\n\n   lines.  To bring the total back to \\(n\\) we delete the axis line \\(L_h\\) (the line\n   \\(y=1\\)).  The points that were on \\(L_h\\) are exactly the points \\((a,1)\\) with\n   \\(2\\le a\\le n\\); each such point lies on the diagonal \\(D_{a+1}\\), and for every\n   \\(a\\) we have either kept that diagonal (if it was not among the \\(k\\) replaced\n   ones) or, if it was replaced, the point \\((a,1)\\) is one of the two endpoints of\n   the diagonal and therefore still lies on the axis line \\(L_v\\) (which we have not\n   removed).  Consequently all points formerly covered by \\(L_h\\) remain covered after\n   the deletion.\n\n   After the deletion we possess exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely the\n   \\(k\\) newly created lines \\(S_{s_i}^{(1)}\\) (or \\(S_{s_i}^{(2)}\\), we keep one of the\n   two for each \\(i\\)) are sunny, while the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.\n   The covering property is preserved because:\n   * points with \\(a=1\\) are on \\(L_v\\);\n   * points with \\(b=1\\) are on a diagonal (or on \\(L_v\\) when the diagonal has been\n     replaced);\n   * points with \\(a,b\\ge2\\) lie on a diagonal; if that diagonal was replaced, the\n     point belongs to one of the two sunny lines that replaced it.\n\n   Thus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have constructed a family of\n   \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points\n   of the required set.\n\n5. **Conclusion** – the inequality \\(k\\le n-2\\) from step 1 is necessary, and the\n   construction of step 4 shows that every integer \\(k\\) satisfying it is attainable.\n   Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nThe three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(1,1),\\qquad P_2=(1,2),\\qquad P_3=(2,1)\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(T_n\\).  Any line through two of them has slope \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=3\\)); therefore a sunny line can\ncontain at most one of the three points.  Consequently at least two of the lines\nthat cover \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) are non‑sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k\\le n-2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A configuration with no sunny lines  \n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n\\[\nL_h:\\;y=1,\\qquad L_v:\\;x=1,\n\\]\n\\[\nD_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=4,5,\\dots ,n+1). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe two axis‑parallel lines have slope \\(0\\) and \\(\\infty\\); each diagonal \\(D_s\\)\nhas slope \\(-1\\).  Hence all lines in (2) are non‑sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_v\\).\n* If \\(a\\neq1\\) but \\(b=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_h\\).\n* If \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\) then \\(a+b\\ge4\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal\n  \\(D_{a+b}\\), which is present because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of (2).  Hence \\(k=0\\) is\nrealizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Replacing a diagonal by two sunny lines  \n\nFix a diagonal \\(D_s\\) with \\(s\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\).  Its intersection with \\(T_n\\) is\n\n\\[\n(1,s-1),\\;(2,s-2),\\dots ,\\;(s-1,1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe two endpoint points \\((1,s-1)\\) and \\((s-1,1)\\) already belong to the axis lines\n\\(L_v\\) and \\(L_h\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\nP_s=\\{(i,s-i)\\mid 2\\le i\\le s-2\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(t=s-2\\;(t\\ge2)\\).  Split \\(P_s\\) into\n\n\\[\nP_s^{(1)}=\\{(i,s-i)\\mid 2\\le i\\le\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor\\},\\qquad\nP_s^{(2)}=\\{(i,s-i)\\mid \\lceil s/2\\rceil\\le i\\le s-2\\}.\n\\]\n\n*All points of \\(P_s^{(1)}\\) are collinear.*  The unique line containing them is\n\n\\[\nS_s^{(1)}:\\; y = \\frac{t}{\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor-1}\\,x\n                -\\frac{t}{\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor-1}+1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*All points of \\(P_s^{(2)}\\) are collinear.*  The unique line containing them is\n\n\\[\nS_s^{(2)}:\\; y = \\frac{t}{\\lceil s/2\\rceil-1}\\,x\n                -\\frac{t}{\\lceil s/2\\rceil-1}+1 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(s\\ge4\\), the denominators \\(\\lfloor s/2\\rfloor-1\\) and\n\\(\\lceil s/2\\rceil-1\\) are positive, so the slopes in (5)–(6) are finite,\nnon‑zero, and different from \\(-1\\).  Hence both \\(S_s^{(1)}\\) and \\(S_s^{(2)}\\) are\nsunny.\n\nA direct substitution shows that every point of \\(P_s^{(1)}\\) satisfies (5) and every\npoint of \\(P_s^{(2)}\\) satisfies (6); consequently\n\n\\[\nP_s\\subset S_s^{(1)}\\cup S_s^{(2)} .\n\\]\n\nTogether with the two endpoint points (which remain on the axis lines) we have\n\n\\[\n(3)\\subset L_v\\cup L_h\\cup S_s^{(1)}\\cup S_s^{(2)} .\n\\]\n\nThus we may **replace** the diagonal \\(D_s\\) by the two sunny lines\n\\(S_s^{(1)},S_s^{(2)}\\) without losing any point of \\(T_n\\).  The replacement\nincreases the total number of lines by one.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for an arbitrary admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).\n\n1. Start from the base configuration (2) (which has \\(k=0\\)).\n2. Choose any \\(k\\) distinct diagonals \\(D_{s_1},\\dots ,D_{s_k}\\) with\n   \\(s_i\\in\\{4,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) and replace each of them by the two sunny lines\n   \\(S_{s_i}^{(1)},S_{s_i}^{(2)}\\) described in step 3.\n\n   After these replacements the family contains  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\;(\\text{axis})+(n-2-k)\\;(\\text{unchanged diagonals})+2k\\;(\\text{sunny lines})\n   =n+k\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines.\n\n3. Delete the horizontal axis line \\(L_h\\) (the line \\(y=1\\)).  \n   The points that were on \\(L_h\\) are exactly the points \\((a,1)\\) with\n   \\(2\\le a\\le n\\).  For each such \\(a\\) the point \\((a,1)\\) lies on the diagonal\n   \\(D_{a+1}\\).  If \\(D_{a+1}\\) was **not** among the replaced diagonals, it is still\n   present and covers the point.  If \\(D_{a+1}\\) was replaced, then \\((a,1)\\) is the\n   endpoint \\((s-1,1)\\) of that diagonal; this endpoint is already covered by the\n   vertical axis line \\(L_v\\), which we have kept.  Hence after deleting \\(L_h\\) every\n   point of \\(T_n\\) remains covered.\n\n   The total number of lines is now exactly \\(n\\).\n\n4. Among the two sunny lines that replace each diagonal we keep **both**; therefore\n   the family contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(k\\) pairs contributed by the\n   replacements) and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the two axis lines, except the deleted\n   \\(L_h\\), and the \\(n-2-k\\) unchanged diagonals).\n\nThus we have produced, for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covering all points of \\(T_n\\) and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion  \n\nThe argument in step 1 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny\nlines.  Steps 2–4 exhibit, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a concrete\nconfiguration satisfying all the requirements.  Therefore the complete set of\nadmissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
