stinctness of the constants \\(t_i\\) guarantees that the lines \\(M_i\\) are distinct.  \n   - Let  \n     \\[\n     D=\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\setminus\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}.\n     \\]\n     Because the \\(s_i\\) are distinct, \\(|D|=n-k\\).  \n   - For every \\(d\\in D\\) keep the diagonal line \\(L_d:x+y=d\\) (non\u2011sunny).  \n\n   The family  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal F_k=\\{M_1,\\dots ,M_k\\}\\cup\\{L_d\\mid d\\in D\\}\n   \\]\n   consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n5. **Verification of coverage.**  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \n   - If \\((a,b)=P_i\\) for some \\(i\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(M_i\\) by construction.  \n   - Otherwise its sum \\(a+b\\) is not among \\(\\{s_1,\\dots ,s_k\\}\\); hence \\(a+b\\in D\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the diagonal line \\(L_{a+b}\\).  \n\n   Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F_k\\).\n\n6. **Upper bound.**  \n   Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\), we cannot have more than \\(n\\) sunny lines; i.e. \\(k\\le n\\).  \n   The construction above works for \\(k=n\\) (take the \\(k=n\\) points from the lemma and omit all diagonal lines), showing that the bound is sharp.\n\nConsequently the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A configuration with no sunny line (\\(k=0\\))\n\nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(2\\le d\\le n+1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\;x+y=d .\n\\]\n\nAll the lines \\(L_d\\) are parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore none of them is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_n\